#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097922 :tutorial/content "How can we calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int} \\sin x \\cos x \\, \\textrm{d}x?$ \n\nNotice that we have the product of sine and cosine with the same argument $(x).$ In such cases, we can use the double-angle formula for sine to solve the integral.\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for sine, which is\n\n$$\n\\sin {2x}=2\\sin x \\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\sin x \\cos x =  \\dfrac{1}{2} \\sin {2x}.\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int \\sin x \\cos x \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin {2x} \\, \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\int \\sin {2x} \\, \\textrm{d}x\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cos {2x}\\right )+C \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=-\\dfrac{1}{4} \\cos {2x}+C.\t\n\t}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can use the double-angle formula for sine to solve any integral that's of the form \n\n$$\n\\int \\sin\\left(ax+b\\right)\\cos(ax+b)\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n Let's take a look at another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c1eb8ee2-db04-591c-b7f9-cd699c4e3573" :tutorial/math-academy-id 878 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097923 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the three double-angle formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(2x) & ={cos}^{2}x-{sin}^{2}x \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}x-1 \\\\  & =1-2{sin}^{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the double-angle formulas for cosine to integrate $\\sin^2{x}$ and $\\cos^2{x}.$ Let's take a look at some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "87755d89-cab8-5737-9e44-ae816aefe23f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 880 :tutorial/title "Using the Double-Angle Formulas for Cosine"}]}
